  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 355 AM CDT Thu Jul 16 2026\n\nPatchy shallow ground fog was beginning to develop over parts of\ncentral IL especially near river valleys and rural cornfields.\nHRRR, RAP13 and GFSLamp show patchy fog and locally dense in\nspots through 12Z especially over Wabash and IL river valleys\nand from Lincoln and SPI eastward and dissipate between 12-13Z.\n\nA 593-594 decameter 500 mb high pressure ridge remains near IL \ntoday though we are seeing a few weak forcing mechanisms at \nplay this afternoon over parts of central/se IL. A weakening \ncold front over ne IL to approach far ne CWA during this morning\nbefore retreating back to the ne this afternoon. Its isolated \nto scattered convection this afternoon to be ne of CWA. \nMeanwhile a weak disturbance over the mid MS river valley has \ndeeper tropical moisture pushing northward over central and \nespecially southeast IL this afternoon. CAMs shows isolated to \nscattered convection developing during this afternoon from SPI \nto Paris south with best chances in se IL where WPC Day1 ERO has\nmarginal risk of excessive rainfall. A few cells could also \nhave gusty winds in southeast IL this afternoon along with \npossible funnel clouds into early evening. Air Quality Alerts \nremain in effect into this evening northern/ne IL into \ncentral/northeast Indiana. HRRR Surface Smoke forecast has it \ngetting about as far sw as a LaSalle to Pontiac to Lafayette \nlater today and tonight before retreating back to the northeast \non Friday. We will likely see some thin elevated smoke aloft \naround cirrus cloud level today and tonight and more prominent \nthe further ne you go. Highs today around 90F to lower 90s with \ndewpoints in the low to mid 70s giving afternoon heat indices in\nupper 90s to lower 100s. Muggy lows overnight in the lower 70s.\n\nDeeper tropical moisture gets further north into central IL on\nFriday and will see isolated to scattered convection especially\nFri afternoon and best chances east of the IL river. HRRR 06Z\nmodel shows less coverage of convection on Friday likely due to\nweaker forcing and working with moderately unstable air mass\nwith weak wind shear. WPC Day2 ERO has marginal risk of\nexcessive rainfall in eastern IL Friday mainly Fri afternoon.\nHREF has localized pockets of 1.5-3 inches in less than 6 hours\npossible in southeast IL this afternoon, and over areas east of\nthe IL river on Friday. Though most areas will receive much \nless rainfall between a tenth and quarter inch southern CWA this\nafternoon, and from IL river east on Friday. Highs Fri in the \nupper 80s to near 90F with afternoon heat indices in upper 90s \nto near 100F. \n\nA stronger northern stream short wave moves into southern\nOntario and eastern Great Lakes late Saturday pushing a cold \nfront southward into northern IL late Sat afternoon and south\nthrough CWA during Sat night. Will see another hot and humid \nday ahead of this front on Saturday with highs in the lower 90s \nand afternoon heat indices 100-105 and getting closer to heat \nadvisory criteria Sat afternoon. Chances of convection to \nincrease during mid/late Sat afternoon into Sat evening with \nhighest pops in eastern IL as cold front approaches late in the \nday. SPC Day3 outlook has marginal risk of severe storms from \nmid to late Sat afternoon into Sat evening over CWA for mainly \ndamaging winds. Locally heavy rains also possible with PWATS \nnear 2 inches. \n\nConvection chances shift south of CWA later Sat night and Sunday\nwith passage of cold front, with seasonable highs in mid to\nupper 80s Sunday and a bit lower dewpoints in the upper\n60s/lower 70s (highest dewpoints in se IL and sw CWA). SW winds\non Monday to bring warmer and more humid air back especially\nwest central/sw IL where highs Mon in lower 90s and heat indices\nMon afternoon likely top 100F. A stronger northern stream short\nwave moving through the Great Lakes region Monday night to push\na stronger cold front se through IL and bring a chance of\nshowers and thunderstorms. Temperatures to cool back during mid\nweek especially Wed/Thu with highs in the low to mid 80s and\nmore comfortable dewpoints in the low to mid 60s. \n\nThe Climate Prediction Center (CPC) 6-10 Day Outlook for July\n21-25 has 33-40% chance of below normal temperatures over\ncentral and northeast IL, with precipitation trending near\nnormal. CPC 8-14 Day Outlook for July 23-29 has temperatures and\nprecipitation trending near normal over central and southeast\nIL. So hopefully we will keep heat waves at bay.",
